  (:adr/body
  "# ADR-2607198200: kotoba guest-grammar has no runtime string interpolation — com-stripe Customer pilot proves the compile→emit→run loop, scopes the 771-repo migration

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-19

## Context

A prior survey counted ~780 repos under orgs/kotoba-lang/ containing files
with the `.kotoba` extension (771 of them the ISIC-taxonomy `com-*` schema
repos, e.g. `com-stripe`, plus 9 core kotoba-lang/kototama/aiueos infra
repos), and treated this as \"repos using the kotoba language\". This ADR
records what actually happened when we tried to migrate one of those repos
(`com-stripe`) to real, executable kotoba-language source and verify it
end-to-end.

## Decision / Findings

1. **`com-stripe/schema/stripe.kotoba` is not kotoba-language source.** It
   fails to parse (`Invalid symbol: //`) against `kotoba.launcher check` —
   it is a distinct C-style `namespace { entity { field: type } }` schema
   DSL (generated by `deepen_actors.py`) that only shares the file
   extension. The 771 `com-*` repos' `.kotoba` files are very likely, on
   inspection of this representative sample, this same schema DSL rather
   than compilable kotoba-language programs — the real business logic for
   these actors lives in `.cljc` (`com-stripe/src/stripe/main.cljc`'s
   `entity-specs`/`handle-create`/`handle-list`/`handle-get` fold).

2. **The current kotoba language (`kotoba-lang/compiler`'s
   `guest-grammar.edn`, status `:p0-p1-in-progress`) has no runtime
   string-building or concatenation builtin.** Strings are immutable
   ≤127-byte compile-time literals only; `kgraph-assert!`/`kgraph-query`
   take the entire EDN triple/query text as one such literal. This means a
   generic `create-customer(email, name, ...)` that asserts arbitrary
   caller-supplied values — the actual shape `stripe.main/handle-create`
   needs — cannot be written in kotoba today. This is a language-maturity
   gap, not a gap in this pilot's implementation.

3. **The compile → wasm emit → execute loop genuinely works**, proven with
   a new file, `orgs/kotoba-lang/com-stripe/schema/customer.kotoba`, porting
   `stripe.main`'s Customer entity (`create-customers`/`get-customer`/
   `list-customers`) to real kotoba source with two compile-time-literal
   canned records standing in for the unavailable dynamic input:
   - `kotoba.launcher check` admits it (4 exports, capability `graph/kotoba`).
   - `kotoba.launcher wasm emit --package-lock kotoba.lock.edn` produces a
     real 777-byte WASM module (4 functions, `kgraph_assert`/`kgraph_query`
     imports).
   - The module was executed **without JVM/Chicory**: a new `nbb` harness
     (`verify_customer_wasm.cljs`, written in nbb per this repo's
     Node-harness convention rather than raw `.mjs`) loads it via Node's
     native `WebAssembly.instantiate` against
     `kotoba-lang/wasm-webcomponent`'s browser-native `src/kgraph.js` host
     imports (the same `actor-host` path as ADR-2607062400) — the same
     kind of host-import port `wasm-webcomponent/test/verify-kgraph.mjs`
     already uses against `kotoba-lang/kotoba`'s own JVM/Chicory fixture,
     here run standalone against a fresh guest.
   - Result: `create-customers` asserted 6 real EAVT facts;
     `get-customer(stripe_cus_0001)` returned its 3 facts; `list-customers()`
     returned `[[stripe_cus_0001 \"ada@example.com\"] [stripe_cus_0002
     \"grace@example.com\"]]`.

## Recommendation

Do not roll this pattern out across the other ~770 `com-*` repos yet as a
production migration — the CRUD shape those actors actually need (accept
arbitrary runtime string field values from a request) is blocked on kotoba
landing runtime string interpolation/concatenation, which `guest-grammar.edn`'s
own maturity roadmap does not yet schedule as `:next` (`typed HIR S1b`,
`signed modules S5`, `full package registry`). Re-evaluate per-repo
migration once string interpolation lands. Until then, this pilot's
compile-time-literal-canned-record pattern is a valid template for further
proof-of-concept ports of the compile→emit→run loop, but is not
behavior-equivalent to the existing `.cljc` actors and should not be
represented as such.

## Evidence

Executable, from `orgs/kotoba-lang/com-stripe` (pilot worktree,
branch `pilot/kotoba-customer-port`):

```
clojure -M:dev -m kotoba.launcher check schema/customer.kotoba --policy schema/customer_policy.edn
=> kotoba.cli/ok? true; exports [create-customers get-customer list-customers main]

clojure -M:dev -m kotoba.launcher wasm emit schema/customer.kotoba \\
  --policy schema/customer_policy.edn --package-lock kotoba.lock.edn --output schema/customer.wasm
=> kotoba.cli/code binary-emitted; 777 bytes; 4 functions; imports kgraph_assert/kgraph_query

nbb schema/verify_customer_wasm.cljs
=> store after create-customers: 6 EAVT facts
=> get-customer(stripe_cus_0001) -> [[:email ada@example.com] [:name \"Ada Lovelace\"] [:balance 0]]
=> list-customers() -> [[stripe_cus_0001 ada@example.com] [stripe_cus_0002 grace@example.com]]
=> OK: customer.wasm ran end-to-end via native WebAssembly + kgraph.js -- no JVM/Chicory involved
```

Negative control, from `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba`:

```
clojure -M:dev -m kotoba.launcher check ../com-stripe/schema/stripe.kotoba
=> Execution error: Invalid symbol: //
```")))
